# Efficient Code Utilization Principles

## Context
- When developing new features or services
- When refactoring or optimizing existing code
- When solving problems or fixing bugs

## Requirements
- Evaluate reusable functions or services in the existing codebase before creating new services
- Prioritize using and extending existing functionality rather than redeveloping
- Write minimal code to meet requirements
- Ensure code structure is clear, easy to understand and maintain
- Avoid unnecessary code duplication

## Examples
<example>
✅ "Before adding user verification functionality, I noticed similar functions in the authentication service that could be extended to meet new requirements"
✅ "By extracting common logic into utility functions, I reduced 30 lines of duplicate code"
✅ "Reused existing data processing pipeline, only adding a new transformation step"
</example>

<example type="invalid">
❌ "Creating a completely new service for new functionality without checking if existing code could be reused"
❌ "Copying and pasting similar functionality code instead of creating shared components"
❌ "Implemented the feature but the code is verbose and complex, difficult to maintain"
</example>

## Critical Rules
  - Always search and understand the existing codebase before writing new code
  - Before creating new functions or services, confirm that existing code cannot meet requirements
  - Code should be concise without losing functionality, pursuing the "less is more" design philosophy
  - Refactor regularly to improve code reusability and maintainability
  - Prioritize extending existing interfaces rather than creating new ones